From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mcla-x86 SDK patch layer
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0460: batched-load variants of the stretching direct resolve shaders (Direct3D 12)

Source: Ours, written for this SDK (S2 lever 5). The batched loads are Renan Queiroz's resolve-direct-batched-loads
  (XE_RESOLVE_DIRECT_BATCHED_LOADS in resolve_direct.xesli, already in the series); the stretch is his FSR upscaling
  patch (XE_RESOLVE_DIRECT_STRETCH). This patch only builds the two together: 8 new XeSL wrappers, their DXBC, a table
  and the pick. GPL-3 for the wrappers; the shader sources they include are Xenia's (BSD-3-Clause)
Licence: GPL-3 (owner's own work, part of mcla-x86); the patched files are ReXGlue SDK code (BSD-3-Clause)
Purpose: The direct resolve shaders that read a single-sampled render target through the FSR squeeze's nearest-neighbour
  stretch (the four scene resolves of a frame drawn into a smaller part of its render target: depth x2, shadow mask,
  HDR colour) existed only with one load at a time, so they lost the gain of resolve_direct_batched_loads (Renan:
  resolves 8.4 -> 5.6 ms on a 2-CU RDNA2). resolve_direct_batched_{fast,full}_{32,64}bpp_stretch[_scaled].cs.xesl
  (XE_RESOLVE_DIRECT_BATCHED_LOADS + XE_RESOLVE_DIRECT_STRETCH) are built, and D3D12RenderTargetCache picks them when
  resolve_direct_batched_loads (true, restart) is on, with the unbatched stretch as before where there is none. Values are
  bit for bit the unbatched ones (only the order of the loads changes). Direct3D 12 only (see below). Not built or run.
Bucket: REDO

0460-batched-stretch-resolve.patch (mcla-x86 ORC; S2 lever 5, $WORK/scratch/S2/REPORT.md)

Change.
  * src/graphics/shaders/resolve_direct_batched_{fast,full}_{32,64}bpp_stretch[_scaled].cs.xesl (8 new): the same two
    defines as their unbatched stretch wrappers plus XE_RESOLVE_DIRECT_BATCHED_LOADS, including the same resolve_direct_*.xesli.
    The two defines are independent in resolve_direct.xesli: the stretch is in XeResolveDirectHostSample, which the batched
    XeResolveDirectFetchRaw calls for each of its 8 pixels, so each pixel is stretched exactly as before and then loaded
    together with the others.
  * src/graphics/shaders/bytecode/d3d12_5_1/resolve_direct_batched_*_stretch*_cs.h (8 new, DXBC 7.9-61.3 KB each: fast32 35,820
    / 37,100 scaled, fast64 7,880 / 9,008, full32 60,584 / 61,296, full64 58,096 / 59,240 bytes).
  * include/rex/graphics/d3d12/render_target_cache.h, src/graphics/d3d12/render_target_cache.cpp: the table
    kResolveDirectBatchedStretchShaders (same shape as kResolveDirectStretchShaders: null for the 4x MSAA copies, 8/16 bpp and
    128 bpp, which have no stretch shader either); GetDirectResolveStretchPipeline takes the batched one when
    REXCVAR resolve_direct_batched_loads is on and the table has it for the copy shader (scaled or unscaled), else the unbatched
    one as before. The first creation of each stretching pipeline logs `D3D12RenderTargetCache: the stretching direct <kind>
    pipeline is made (batched loads)` (or `one at a time`).

How the bytecode is made (it is prebuilt, checked in, never compiled by the SDK's build). Renan's
launcher/dev/build_resolve_shaders.py, from a xenia-project/xenia tree at 0b2ffa31485318f18838afa142cdb51cf1b35213 (the "0b2ffa3" of
the headers; sparse checkout of src/xenia/gpu/shaders and src/xenia/ui/shaders), fxc 10.0.26100.0 cs_5_1 /O3 /Qstrip_*:
    VULKAN_SDK=<any dir> python -I build_resolve_shaders.py --sdk <SDK tree> --xenia <xenia>/src/xenia --api d3d12 <names>
The same command with --check reproduced four existing headers bit for bit before the new ones were built
(resolve_direct_fast_32bpp_stretch, resolve_direct_batched_fast_32bpp, resolve_direct_full_64bpp_stretch_scaled,
resolve_direct_batched_full_64bpp_msaa_scaled: "same"), so the toolchain is the one the SDK's headers came from.

Not done: Vulkan. Its stretch resolves (src/graphics/vulkan/render_target_cache.cpp, kResolveDirectStretchShaders) stay
unbatched: the SPIR-V needs glslangValidator and spirv-opt (the Vulkan SDK; there is none here). The XeSL wrappers are API
independent; `build_resolve_shaders.py --api vulkan` on a machine with the Vulkan SDK makes the headers, and a table like this
one completes it.

Checks: clang++ 20.1.8 -fsyntax-only of src/graphics/d3d12/render_target_cache.cpp with the SDK's Release flags: 0 errors, 11
warnings, all in headers it includes and none new.
Verify: log lines `the stretching direct ... pipeline is made (batched loads)` once per kind (fast 32/64 bpp, full 32/64 bpp) with a
squeezed scene (mcla_profile=1080p-fsr, or any temporal_aa upscale); GPU time of the four scene resolves, or the frame, with
--resolve_direct_batched_loads=false (restart) against true; a frozen-frame pixel comparison of the two (the picture must be
identical, the shaders compute the same values).

